<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-quantities-expressions-and-equivalence" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Quantities, Expressions, and Equivalence</title>

    <exercise xml:id="opening-example-ariana">
        <title>Opening Example</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                We start this section with an example.
            </p>
            <p>
                Ariana is taking a picture of a friend on her phone. She experiments with different camera settings until she's happy with the photograph.
            </p>

            <image source="photos/ariana.png" width="70%">
            <shortdescription>
                Ariana's friend appears on a phone screen.
            </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <p>
                In algebra, we usually work with quantities that vary. By <term>quantity</term>, we mean something you can measure. There are many quantities Ariana might consider when taking her photo. Some of them change while others do not. For instance, the <em>width of her friend's face on the screen</em> is one quantity that varies. We can measure this width and watch it change as we zoom in and out. On the other hand,  the <em>overall width of the screen</em> is a quantity that does not change.
            </p>
            <p>
                Identify at least two other quantities that vary and two other quantities that remain fixed.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>Quantities that vary:</p>
            </statement>
            <response/>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>Fixed quantities:</p>
            </statement>
            <response/>
        </task>
    </exercise>
    <assemblage>
        <title>Student Thinking</title>
        <p>
            Here's what some other students had to say about this problem.
        </p>
        <sidebyside widths="48% 48%">
            <stack>
                <p>
                    <image source="photos/sidney-mockup-3.png" width="100%">
                    </image>
                </p>

                <p>
                    You know, even if she zooms in on her camera, the width of her friend's actual face in real-life is not going to change. So one fixed quantity is the actual width of her friend's face. I guess the height of her friend's face is also fixed in real-life but the height of the face on the screen will vary as you zoom in and out.
                </p>
            </stack>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    <image source="photos/ericka-mockup-3.png" width="100%">
                    </image>
                </p>

                <p>
                    I think maybe the distance from the camera could vary like if she moved closer to or further away from her friend? Maybe, also something about the brightness of the photo like if they turned on some extra lights in the room?
                </p>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>
    </assemblage>
    <p>
        Sidney and Erika provided some great examples of quantities that vary. The actual width of Ariana’s friend's face does not change. However, when Ariana zooms in and out, the width of her friend’s face on the screen does change. Some other quantities that vary include the pixel depth and the brightness of the image. Another fixed quantity is the dimensions of the screen on her phone.
    </p>
    <p>
        Algebra is full of quantities like these—some that change, some that stay fixed, and others whose values we don't yet know. We use letters like <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> to represent all these different kinds of quantities, not just the ones we don’t yet know. To master algebra, you will need to get comfortable working with letters in all these different ways. As a starting point, consider how we use letters in algebraic expressions.
    </p>
    <definition xml:id="def-algebraic-expression">
        <statement>
            <p>
                An <term>algebraic expression</term> is what we get when we combine numbers and letters using operations like addition, subtraction, and multiplication.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
        For instance, <m>3 + x</m>,  <m>\;6A - 7</m>,  <m>\;p {\cdot} p + 4q</m>,  and  <m>\;\frac{45s}{t}</m> are all algebraic expressions. Even a single number or letter on its own (just <m>x</m> or just <m>9</m>) can be an algebraic expression.
    </p>
    <p>
        An expression like <m>S + C</m> tells us how to calculate a quantity (add <m>S</m> and <m>C</m>) but it also represents the quantity we get from that calculation. For instance, if <m>S</m> is the amount of money in your savings account and <m>C</m> is the amount of money in your checking account, then <m>S + C</m> is the total money you have in both accounts.
    </p>
    <p>
        To really understand this expression, you need to be able to do three things:
        <ol>
            <li>Calculate <m>S + C</m> by plugging in different values for <m>S</m> and <m>C</m> and adding them up.</li>
            <li>Imagine the quantities <m>S</m> and <m>C</m> increasing and decreasing.</li>
            <li>Interpret the meaning of <m>S + C</m> as the total money in both accounts.</li>
        </ol>
        Most people can do number one, but struggle with two and three. Unfortunately, two and three are often the more important skills so we are going to take a moment to practice them.
    </p>
    <exercise xml:id="ariana-revisited">
        <title>Returning to Ariana's Photograph</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider again the photograph that Ariana is taking on her phone. We'll use four letters to describe what we see:
                <ul>
                    <li><m>W</m> is the width of the photograph’s frame;</li>
                    <li><m>F</m> is the width of the friend's face at 1× zoom;</li>
                    <li><m>z</m> is the zoom factor, and</li>
                    <li><m>L</m> is the distance from the left edge of the frame to the left edge of the face.</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <image source="photos/ariana.png" width="70%">
            <shortdescription>
                Ariana's friend appears on a phone screen.
            </shortdescription>
            </image>
            <p>
                Consider the expression <m>L + zF</m>. Experiment with the two sliders and observe how the bars A-D change.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which bar do you think represents <m>L+zF</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <choices>
                <choice><p>A</p></choice>
                <choice><p>B</p></choice>
                <choice correct="yes"><p>C</p></choice>
                <choice><p>D</p></choice>
            </choices>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What quantity does the expression <m>L + zF</m> represent? Explain your thinking.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <response/>
        </task>
    </exercise>
    <assemblage>
        <title>Student Thinking</title>
        <sidebyside widths="48% 48%">
            <stack>
                <p><em>Sofia</em></p>

                <p>
                    As we zoom in, her face should get wider on the screen. By the time we get to 2x zoom, her face should have width <m>2F</m>. I think that means that <m>zF</m> is the width of the face at the current zoom level and <m>L + zF</m> should get bigger as we zoom in. I noticed that both A and C get bigger as we zoom in so I think it's one of those two quantities.
                </p>
            </stack>
            <stack>
                <p><em>Jefferson</em></p>

                <p>
                    If we point the camera to the right without changing the zoom, her face gets closer to the left side of the picture, so <m>L</m> gets smaller. That means <m>L + zF</m> gets smaller too. Because of this, I think <m>L + zF</m> has something to do with the distance from the left side of the photo?
                </p>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>        
    </assemblage>

</section>